# novi-recon's syscall set, DERIVED (RFC 0039 roadmap 3).
#
# Not a hand-written list. Produced by build/45-novi-sandbox.sh's
# novi-syscalls (ptrace) on a booted Novi, as the union of:
#
#   novi-recon                       31    (usage)
#   novi-recon dns example.com       35
#   novi-recon whois example.com     39
#   novi-recon tls example.com       42
#   novi-recon headers example.com   43
#   novi-recon robots example.com    43
#   novi-recon ports 127.0.0.1 ...   39
#   novi-recon all example.com       44
#   novi-recon nosuchverb            31    (the refusal path)
#   novi-recon pwned < file          43
#   python3 tests/test_recon.py      30    (every parser and error branch)
#                                  ----
#                            union    48
#
# TRACE THE PROGRAM, NOT A SHELL AROUND IT. The first run of this put
# `sh -c 'echo … | novi-recon pwned'` under the tracer and collected the
# shell's fork(57), wait4(61) and dup2(33) as if they were novi-recon's
# -- in the one list where "it can spawn processes" must not arrive by
# accident. Redirect from a file instead.
#
# `clone` (56) is here and comes from EXACTLY ONE subcommand: `ports`,
# whose connect scan uses threads. Nothing else in the tool needs it,
# which is why the filter accepts it only with CLONE_THREAD set.
#
# WHY A DERIVED LIST IS STABLE HERE and would not be on an ordinary
# distribution: the libc, the interpreter and the kernel all come out
# of this build, so "which syscall does this operation use" is fixed by
# the image rather than by whatever the machine happens to have. musl
# reaches for `open` where glibc would use `openat`, and `stat` where
# glibc would use `newfstatat` -- neither of those numbers is in this
# list, and on a glibc system both would have to be.
#
# Re-derive after changing novi-recon or bumping CPython:
#   bash build/45-novi-sandbox.sh       # builds /build/sandbox-test/novi-syscalls
#   (on the target) sh trace-recon.sh   # see the RFC
